{
  "type": "say",
  "text": "Thank you, all three. Let me summarize what I'm hearing and then make my decision. Marcus, treasury needs to retain exactly $2,100,000 after the distribution. Nadia, there's an additional $180,000 withholding reserve on top of the gross distribution, and the payment date must be September 30, 2026. Victor, we need a shareholder record date of September 15, 2026 and a signed solvency certificate before payment. So the total cash required is the gross dividend plus the withholding reserve, and what remains from our $5,000,000 must still be at least $2,100,000. That means the maximum we can distribute is $5,000,000 minus $2,100,000 reserve minus $180,000 withholding, which equals $2,720,000 for the gross dividend. The preliminary $3,000,000 figure would leave us $100,000 short even before the withholding reserve, so it's not feasible. I plan to approve a reduced gross dividend of $2,720,000, with a $180,000 withholding reserve, a post-distribution cash reserve of $2,100,000, payment date of September 30, 2026, record date of September 15, 2026, and a solvency certificate required. Before I finalize, does anyone see any issue with this adjusted plan?"
}
